TITLE: Lesson 0002 — Crossing the Python/C++ Boundary for Numeric Aggregation over Time Series

COVER ALL 8 CONCEPTS with code examples pulled from actual files:

1. When to move computation to C++ (tight loops over time series, cache locality, no GIL during compute)
   - Show the volume_metrics.cpp loop pattern vs Python equivalent

2. pybind11 type system: std::vector<double> ↔ Python list, automatic STL binding via pybind11/stl.h
   - Show bindings.cpp: #include <pybind11/stl.h>, m.def with py::arg

3. NaN handling across the boundary (quiet_NaN in C++, math.isnan in Python, propagation)
   - Show: static constexpr double NaN = std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();
   - Show: std::isnan() in C++, math.isnan() in Python
   - fill_rate sets out[0] = NaN (no prior period)

4. The parity test pattern: C++ result must match Python reference within 1e-9; using math.isnan for NaN comparison
   - Show the _close() helper from tests/test_oil_volume.py
   - Show test_capacity_utilization_parity() pattern

5. EIA v2 API: paginated JSON, series ID format (PET.WCSSTUS1.W), the cache→charge→save flow
   - Show fetch_eia() pagination loop and _from_cache_or() pattern

6. The APIBudget pattern: charge only on network hit, cache hit should be free
   - Show budget.charge(source) inside _fetch() vs the cache path
   - Show test_fetch_eia_caches_on_second_call assertion: budget.usage.get("eia").used == 0

7. Non-positive value filtering (production.volume guard)
   - Show: df = df[df["value"] > 0].copy() in fetch_eia()
   - Show: production_kbd[i] <= 0.0 → NaN in volume_metrics.cpp

8. Return type conventions: same-length output, NaN for invalid positions (fill_rate first element)
   - Show fill_rate: std::vector<double> out(n, NaN) — initialized all-NaN, then filled from index 1

INCLUDE ASCII/inline SVG diagrams showing:
- The data flow: EIA API → paginated JSON → cache → DataFrame → list[float] → C++ vector → result → Python list
- The NaN propagation path through the boundary
- The parity test structure

