217	<h2 id="why-cpp">1. Why C++ Extensions in a Python Trading System</h2>
218	
219	<p>Python is excellent for strategy research: expressive, ecosystem-rich, and fast to iterate on. But in HFT the bottleneck is often a tight inner loop — rolling statistics, order-book aggregation, position marking — that runs millions of times per second. NumPy vectorises many of those, but:</p>
220	
221	<ul>
222	  <li><strong>Memory layout control.</strong> C++ lets you choose SoA vs AoS, align buffers on cache-line boundaries, and avoid the overhead of Python object headers (56 bytes per Python float vs 8 bytes for a C <code>double</code>).</li>
223	  <li><strong>SIMD intrinsics.</strong> You can call <code>_mm256_fmadd_ps</code> directly or let the compiler auto-vectorise with <code>-O3 -march=native</code>. NumPy does this behind ufuncs, but you lose that control for custom kernels.</li>
224	  <li><strong>No GIL contention.</strong> A C extension can release the GIL (<code>py::gil_scoped_release</code>) and run on multiple threads without serialising through the interpreter lock.</li>
225	  <li><strong>Predictable latency.</strong> Python's garbage collector introduces pause jitter. C++ with arena allocators or pre-allocated pools eliminates it.</li>
226	  <li><strong>Deterministic type promotion.</strong> Numpy sometimes silently upcasts; C++ template specialisations make promotion explicit and compile-checked.</li>
227	</ul>
228	
229	<div class="callout info">
230	  <strong>This repo's case</strong>
231	  The <code>rolling_mean</code> kernel is a proof of concept. The same pattern — a tight loop, pre-allocated output buffer, a <code>PYBIND11_MODULE</code> facade — scales to OHLCV bar construction, VWAP, exponential smoothing, or custom order-book aggregation without changing the build structure.
232	</div>
