234	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
235	<h2 id="pybind11-mechanics">2. How pybind11 Works</h2>
236	
237	<p>pybind11 is a header-only C++ library that maps C++ types, functions, and classes to Python objects at compile time using C++ templates and constexpr machinery. There is no code generation step — the mapping is entirely in headers.</p>
238	
239	<h3>The module macro</h3>
240	<p><code>PYBIND11_MODULE(name, m)</code> expands to a special <code>extern "C"</code> function that Python's import machinery calls when it loads the shared library. The name you pass (<code>oil_volume_cpp</code>) must exactly match the file name Python looks for (<code>oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so</code>).</p>
241	
242	<h3>Type casters</h3>
243	<p>When Python calls <code>oil_volume_cpp.rolling_mean([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 3)</code>, pybind11's <em>type caster</em> system converts:</p>
244	<ul>
245	  <li>Python <code>list[float]</code> → <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> (via <code>#include &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code>)</li>
246	  <li>Python <code>int</code> → C++ <code>int</code> (built-in caster)</li>
247	  <li>Return: <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> → Python <code>list[float]</code> (copy, one allocation)</li>
248	</ul>
249	<p>Type casters are resolved at compile time; there is no runtime reflection. If you try to pass a <code>str</code> where a <code>double</code> is expected, Python raises a <code>TypeError</code> at the boundary.</p>
250	
251	<h3>py::arg</h3>
252	<p><code>py::arg("values")</code> and <code>py::arg("window")</code> attach keyword-argument names. Without them the function can only be called positionally. With them, Python callers can write <code>rolling_mean(values=[1,2,3], window=3)</code>.</p>
253	
254	<h3>return_value_policy</h3>
255	<p>When a C++ function returns a raw pointer or reference to memory it owns, pybind11 needs to know whether Python should keep that memory alive, make a copy, or take ownership. The default policy (<code>return_value_policy::automatic</code>) copies value types (like <code>std::vector</code>) and references class types. For the <code>rolling_mean</code> function the vector is returned by value so the default is correct — pybind11 moves it into a Python list and the C++ vector is destroyed.</p>
256	
257	<table>
258	  <tr><th>Policy</th><th>Effect</th><th>When to use</th></tr>
259	  <tr><td><code>copy</code></td><td>Always deep-copy</td><td>Returning internal state you don't want mutated</td></tr>
260	  <tr><td><code>reference</code></td><td>Borrow (no lifetime management)</td><td>Returning a reference to a long-lived C++ object — dangerous</td></tr>
261	  <tr><td><code>take_ownership</code></td><td>Python owns the pointer</td><td>Heap-allocated object from <code>new</code></td></tr>
262	  <tr><td><code>automatic</code> (default)</td><td>Copy for values, reference for pointers</td><td>Most functions returning by value</td></tr>
263	</table>
